abstract: >-
  A reproducible pilot (Python 3 standard library only, synthetic data) and a reusable
  toolkit (release_audit) for measuring how much small sets of structured findings,
  passed between workflow stages, reveal about an attribute none of them states. A
  fictional two-stage household workflow releases intake and follow-up findings while a
  three-level reserve band stays hidden. On 3,000 test households the pilot compares one
  release, two linked releases, a declared coarsening of the linked releases, and the
  report-checklist answers alone, against attackers trained on 6,000 labels and against
  exactly computed Bayes-optimal ceilings and mutual information, with a task-tied utility
  measure from a strict, abstaining report renderer. The toolkit takes a JSON description
  of a pipeline and reports exact leakage, a per-field information budget, Pareto-optimal
  release designs and, for labelled records, cross-validated estimates.
